== --help
Run any coding agent on any host, and use it as if it ran here

Usage:
  <name>            opens the launcher
  <name> [command]

Examples:
  <name> setup                       # connect a host, step by step
  <name> new my-vps --agent claude   # start an agent, and attach to it
  <name> attach my-vps/auth-fix      # go back to a session

Sessions:
  new         Start a session on a host, and attach to it
  shell       Open a shell beside a session
  attach      Attach to a session
  resume      Start a session's agent again after it ended, and attach to it
  fork        Copy a sandbox into independent sessions
  rewind      List disk checkpoints or return to one
  ls          List sessions on every host, or on one
  show        Describe one session: its agent, state, folder and attach command
  stop        Stop a sandbox and keep its work and conversation
  rm          Delete a session, after a question
  rename      Rename a session
  send        Type text into a session without attaching, then press Enter
  ports       List the TCP ports a session's processes listen on
  forward     Forward chosen session ports, or one laptop port in reverse
  pull        Bring a session's branch into the git repository you are in
  cp          Copy files between this computer and a running sandbox
  events      Read recent session events, or follow them as they happen

Hosts:
  host        Add, create, list, test and remove hosts

Images:
  image       List, pull, save and remove microvm root images

Policy:
  policy      Read and change sandbox policy at any layer

Agents:
  agents      List the agents, install one on a host, and log in to it

Setup and help:
  setup       Set up step by step: a host, its agents, and their logins
  ssh-config  Prepare the local SSH configuration for sandbox tools
  doctor      Check this machine and its terminal
  debug       Record why a session goes wrong: debug files, profiles and traces
  config      Show the configuration
  demo        Try the launcher with sample data (nothing connects anywhere)
  version     Print the version
  completion  Print the completion script for bash, zsh, fish or PowerShell
  help        Show the help of a command or a topic

Flags:
      --debug categories[=attach,net,memory,launcher,runtime]
                turn on the debug mode, with every category, or with the
                categories listed: attach, net, memory, launcher, runtime.
                A list binds only in the = form, such as --debug=net
      --debug-file file
                write the client's debug records to this file. On its own,
                it turns on every category (<NAME>_DEBUG_FILE does the same)
  -h, --help
                help for <name>
      --no-color
                print no colour (NO_COLOR does the same)
      --no-input
                ask nothing: a question that needs an answer fails with
                exit code 4, and names the flag that answers it
                (<NAME>_NO_INPUT=1 does the same)
      --version
                print the version

Additional help topics:
  concepts    The words that this tool uses
  exit-codes  What each exit code means

Use "<name> [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== help concepts
The words that <name> uses, and what each one means.

  layer              One source of settings and rules. Narrower layers override
                     broader ones.
  profile            A named policy layer selected after repository sections.
  rule               An allow or deny for one door and destination.
  door               A controlled way through a sandbox wall, such as the
                     network.
  preset             A built-in network policy: locked, balanced or open.
  sandbox            A session workspace behind a wall with explicit grants.
  wall               The boundary around a sandbox.
  level              The wall strength: none, process, gvisor or microvm.
  floor              The minimum wall level saved for a host.
  tier               The laptop's limit on what a host may receive.
  host               The Linux machine, VM or container that runs sessions.
  helper             The program on each host that runs its sessions.
  session            A terminal on a host, which keeps running when you leave.
  address            <host>/<session>, a unique session name, or - for the last
                     attached.
  agent              A coding agent, such as Claude Code or Codex.
  launcher           The screen of hosts and sessions that <name> opens.
  attach and detach  Attach shows a session in this terminal. Ctrl+\ detaches by
                     default, and attach.detach_key can change it.
  worktree           A session's own git worktree and branch (new --worktree).
  secret             A value from a command on this computer, such as a token.
  alert              A message from a host when a session needs you or fails.
  provider script    A script of yours that starts, stops and checks a host.
